window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"u": "index.html", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "", "x": "RingDotNet Call any .NET class from Ring with natural syntax. Build Windows Forms and WPF programs, web servers with ASP.NET Core, and databases with Entity Framework \u2014 all in Ring. Get started See the applications Function index 112 Namespaces 4,588 Wrapped classes 32 Forms samples 10 WPF samples 45 Applications 24 Test files"}, {"u": "index.html#a-first-taste", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "A first taste", "x": "A first taste # load \"dotnetlib.ring\" dnImport(\"System\") dnImport(\"System.Text\") oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") oSB.Append(\", \").Append(\"Ring!\") ? oSB.ToString() # Hello, Ring! ? oSB.Length # 12 ? new Math().Max(3,9) # 9 ? new Math().Sqrt(144) # 12 The classes are real Ring classes with the same method names as the .NET ones \u2014 like RingQt for Qt \u2014 so code completion, inheritance and ordinary Ring syntax just work. There is no eval and no string-based dispatch in your code."}, {"u": "index.html#what-is-inside", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "What is inside", "x": "What is inside # \ud83d\udd0c Natural syntax oObj.Method(), oObj.Property = x, enums as strings, events with setClickEvent(). \ud83d\udcda The class library Strings, dates, files, regex, networking, crypto, XML, JSON, reflection, LINQ and more. \ud83d\udd01 Callbacks and events Pass Ring code wherever .NET expects a delegate, as RingQt does with events. \ud83e\ude9f Windows Forms All controls wrapped, plus short helpers: uiForm(), uiButton(), UiCanvas for drawing. \ud83c\udfa8 WPF Windows, XAML, data binding, shapes, brushes and animation. \ud83c\udf10 ASP.NET Core A Kestrel web server whose routes are Ring functions. JSON in one call. \ud83d\uddc4\ufe0f Entity Framework Co"}, {"u": "index.html#see-it-running", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "See it running", "x": "See it running #"}, {"u": "index.html#how-the-package-is-organised", "t": "RingDotNet", "h": "How the package is organised", "x": "How the package is organised # Folder Contents bin/ ring_dotnet.dll (the C extension), the managed bridge, ASP.NET and EF assemblies libraries/dotnetlib/ The Ring library: functions, the generated wrapper classes, GUI/WPF/web/data helpers extensions/ringdotnet/ C source of the extension and C# source of the bridge, web server and data layer samples/ 32 Windows Forms samples, 10 WPF samples and console samples applications/ Complete programs tests/ Automated tests ( tests/run.sh ) tools/ The class generators ( gendotnet.ring , wrapassembly.ring ) and the manifest Tip New here? Read Getting star"}, {"u": "getting-started.html", "t": "Getting started", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "getting-started.html#requirements", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Requirements", "x": "Requirements # You need Why Ring 1.27 or newer The language. The package is a normal Ring package. .NET 6 or newer (tested with .NET 9) The runtime that RingDotNet hosts inside the Ring process. Windows Desktop runtime Only for Windows Forms and WPF programs (it is part of the .NET Desktop runtime installer). ASP.NET Core runtime Only for web programs ( dnUseWeb() ). Note The package is built and tested on Windows with Ring 1.28 and .NET 9. The C extension and the managed bridge are portable; the Windows Forms and WPF parts are Windows-only by nature."}, {"u": "getting-started.html#install", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Install", "x": "Install # Install the package with the Ring package manager, or use the folder directly. The folder contains everything: the extension DLL ( bin/ring_dotnet.dll ), the managed assemblies and the Ring library. # use the folder directly: the extension DLL must be found by Ring (put bin on PATH) set PATH=B:\\newpro\\ringdotnet\\bin;%PATH% ring samples\\UsingDotNet\\test01_hello.ring When the package is installed into the Ring folder, load \"dotnetlib.ring\" is enough. From a program inside the package folder load the library by its path: load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\""}, {"u": "getting-started.html#your-first-program", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Your first program", "x": "Your first program # load \"dotnetlib.ring\" ? dnVersion() # the .NET runtime that is running dnImport(\"System\") dnImport(\"System.Text\") oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") oSB.Append(\", \").Append(\"Ring!\") ? oSB.ToString() ? oSB.Length ? new Math().Max(3,9) Runtime : .NET 9.0.15 Hello from .NET (Console.WriteLine) Math.Max(3,9) = 9 Math.Sqrt(144) = 12 Math.PI = 3.14 Environment.OS = Microsoft Windows NT 10.0.26200.0 Hello, Ring! Length = 12 Type = System.Text.StringBuilder Count = 2 one two Caught: DotNet Error: System.FormatException: The input string 'abc' was not in a correct format. The output "}, {"u": "getting-started.html#the-startup-modes", "t": "Getting started", "h": "The startup modes", "x": "The startup modes # The first .NET call starts the runtime. If your program needs more than the base library, call one of these before any other .NET call : Call Starts Use it for (none) .NET base runtime Console programs, files, networking, JSON, XML, reflection, LINQ dnUseDesktop() Windows Desktop runtime, Ring thread becomes STA Windows Forms, WPF dnUseWeb() ASP.NET Core runtime, loads the web server WebServer ( ASP.NET Core ) dnUseAll() Desktop and ASP.NET Core together A GUI program that also serves HTTP dnUseData() Loads the Entity Framework assemblies RingDatabase , RingEntityDb ( Entit"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#importing-namespaces", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Importing namespaces", "x": "Importing namespaces # The wrapper classes are loaded on demand , one namespace at a time, so a program only pays for what it uses: dnImport(\"System.IO\") # File, Directory, Path, StreamReader ... dnImport(\"System.Text.RegularExpressions\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Forms\") # also loads the GUI helpers (uiForm, uiButton ...) Or, like C#, with using \u2014 the same thing, shorter. Each item must be a text; anything else is an error: using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" \"System.IO\" } If a method returns an object whose class belongs to a namespace you did not import, that namespace is imported automatically, "}, {"u": "getting-started.html#your-first-window", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Your first window", "x": "Your first window # # A button that counts its clicks load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseDesktop() dnImport(\"System.Drawing\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Forms\") nClicks = 0 oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) oCount = uiLabel(oForm,\"0 clicks\",20,15,220,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,50,100,32,\"clicked()\") uiTest(oForm,\"selfTest()\") uiRun(oForm) uiEnd() # -------------------------------------------------------------------------- func clicked nClicks++ oCount.Text = \"\" + nClicks + \" clicks\" func selfTest oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformCl"}, {"u": "getting-started.html#run-the-tests", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Run the tests", "x": "Run the tests # Every feature has a test, and every sample and application can test itself: sh tests/run.sh B:/ring/bin/ring.exe # runs tests, samples and applications ring applications/Calculator/main.ring --selftest # drives the form and prints SELFTEST OK Read Testing for how the self tests work."}, {"u": "getting-started.html#where-to-go-next", "t": "Getting started", "h": "Where to go next", "x": "Where to go next # Core concepts : how .NET names, overloads, properties, enums and events look in Ring. Windows Forms and the applications . ASP.NET Core and Entity Framework Core for servers and databases. Gotchas & limits before you write a long program."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html", "t": "How it works", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-layers", "t": "How it works", "h": "The layers", "x": "The layers # Your Ring program oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hi\") \u00b7 oForm.Text = \"Hello\" \u00b7 oBtn.setClickEvent(\"...\") dotnetlib (Ring library) generated wrapper classes \u00b7 dnImport \u00b7 dnCallback \u00b7 gui.ring \u00b7 wpf.ring \u00b7 web.ring \u00b7 data.ring ring_dotnet.dll (C extension) starts the runtime through hostfxr \u00b7 sends requests \u00b7 runs Ring callbacks RingDotNet.Bridge.dll (C#): reflection, handle table, type conversion, delegates .NET class library WinForms \u00b7 WPF \u00b7 ASP.NET \u00b7 EF Your DLLs and NuGet packages"}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-extension-and-the-bridge", "t": "How it works", "h": "The extension and the bridge", "x": "The extension and the bridge # ring_dotnet.dll is a normal Ring extension written in C. When the first .NET call is made it: finds the installed .NET runtime and starts it inside the Ring process with hostfxr (no separate process, no IPC), loads RingDotNet.Bridge.dll , a small C# assembly, keeps one entry point: dotnet_request(operation, data) . The bridge uses reflection. A Ring call such as oSB.Append(\", \") becomes a request call [handle, \"Append\", \", \"] ; the bridge finds the right overload from the argument types, invokes it, and returns the result."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#handles", "t": "How it works", "h": "Handles", "x": "Handles # .NET objects never leave .NET. Ring holds a handle (a number) inside a DotNetObject , and the bridge keeps the real object in a table. The generated wrapper classes are DotNetObject s with typed methods, so oSB.Append(...) and oSB.callMethod(\"Append\",[...]) do the same thing."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#the-wire-format", "t": "How it works", "h": "The wire format", "x": "The wire format # Values travel as a compact tagged text: s5:Hello (string), n2:42 (number), l... (list), o3:17 (object handle), c... (callback), e... (error). Numbers that are not whole travel as the 8 bytes of the double, so there is no loss of precision . Binary data ( byte[] ) travels as a Ring string."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#generated-wrapper-classes", "t": "How it works", "h": "Generated wrapper classes", "x": "Generated wrapper classes # tools/gendotnet.ring reads the manifest ( tools/dotnetmanifest.ring ) and uses reflection to write one Ring class per .NET class into libraries/dotnetlib/generated/ . These files are plain Ring source with real inheritance, properties (as attributes with getX / setX ), events and enums. dnImport(\"System.IO\") loads one namespace on demand. A class index lets the library import the right namespace automatically when a returned object has a type that is not loaded yet."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#results-are-wrapped-by-their-real-type", "t": "How it works", "h": "Results are wrapped by their real type", "x": "Results are wrapped by their real type # When a method returns an object, the bridge sends the list of its type and base types. dnWrapObject looks for the most specific generated class (importing its namespace if needed) and creates it. If no class exists the result is a plain DotNetObject \u2014 use callMethod and getProp on it."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#callbacks-ring-code-called-from-net", "t": "How it works", "h": "Callbacks: Ring code called from .NET", "x": "Callbacks: Ring code called from .NET # dnCallback(\"...\") passes Ring code where .NET expects a delegate. The bridge builds a delegate of the exact type with a compiled expression; when .NET invokes it, the C extension runs the Ring function on the Ring thread and returns its result. Callbacks run synchronously during a call, which is why events from timers on other threads are ignored (see Gotchas )."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#threads", "t": "How it works", "h": "Threads", "x": "Threads # Thread What runs there The Ring thread All Ring code, all calls into .NET and all callbacks. With dnUseDesktop() it is an STA thread and runs the message loop in Application.Run() . .NET threads Kestrel connections, tasks, timers. They never call Ring directly. The web server queues requests and Ring handles them in serveOne() ."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#runtime-modes", "t": "How it works", "h": "Runtime modes", "x": "Runtime modes # The runtime is configured with one of four runtimeconfig.json files: base ( RingDotNet.Bridge ), desktop ( RingDotNet.Desktop ), web ( RingDotNet.Web ) and all ( RingDotNet.All ). dnUseDesktop() , dnUseWeb() and dnUseAll() select the file before the runtime starts. dnUseData() only loads the Entity Framework assemblies; an AssemblyResolve handler in the bridge finds them next to it."}, {"u": "how-it-works.html#packages", "t": "How it works", "h": "Packages", "x": "Packages # File Role ring_dotnet.dll C extension RingDotNet.Bridge.dll reflection bridge RingDotNet.Web.dll request queue and Kestrel host RingDotNet.Data.dll RingDb and RingTypedDb (Entity Framework) Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore*.dll , e_sqlite3.dll ... Entity Framework Core and SQLite"}, {"u": "concepts.html", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "concepts.html#classes-with-the-same-names-as-net", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Classes with the same names as .NET", "x": "Classes with the same names as .NET # After dnImport(\"Namespace\") every public type of that namespace is a Ring class with the same short name and the same members: dnImport(\"System.Text\") oSB = new StringBuilder(\"Hello\") # use parentheses: Ring calls init() only with them oSB.Append(\", Ring\").Append(\"!\") # methods that return a wrapped class return that class ? oSB.ToString() ? oSB.Length # properties and fields are attributes new Console().WriteLine(\"Hi\") # static members are called on an instance ? new Math().Max(3,9) Hello from .NET Max(3,9) = 9 Sqrt(144) = 12 PI = 3.14 Hello, Ring! Length"}, {"u": "concepts.html#naming-rules", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Naming rules", "x": "Naming rules # Ring has no method overloading, so overloads get predictable names: .NET Ring name Rule Append() , Append(string) , Append(char, int) Append , Append_1 , Append_2 The plain name is the parameterless form when the type has one, otherwise the shortest overload. The others are Name_N where N is the number of parameters. Console.WriteLine(\"x\") WriteLine(\"x\") Write* methods keep the shortest overload with a parameter. Next() , Next(int) , Next(int,int) Next , Next_1 , Next_2 Name_N counts parameters. Serialize(value, options = null) Serialize(value) , Serialize_2(value, options) A me"}, {"u": "concepts.html#properties-and-fields-are-attributes", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Properties and fields are attributes", "x": "Properties and fields are attributes # .NET properties are Ring attributes. Ring calls the generated getName() / setName(value) for you, so reading and writing is natural, also inside { } : oForm = new Form() oForm.Text = \"Hello\" oForm.Width = 400 ? oForm.Controls.Count # chained: Controls returns a ControlCollection oForm { Height = 300 Text += \"!\" } Assigning a read-only property raises an error. Properties whose names are Ring keywords ( From , To , Step , On ...) are not attributes: use getFrom() / setFrom(value) . Length = 5 Truncated = Hel Math.PI = 3.14 Path sep = \\ Count = 2 Capacity ="}, {"u": "concepts.html#enums-are-strings", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Enums are strings", "x": "Enums are strings # Enum values are strings in both directions : oForm.WindowState = \"Maximized\" oForm.FormBorderStyle = \"FixedDialog\" oDate = new DateTime(2024,12,25) ? oDate.DayOfWeek # \"Wednesday\" oLabel.TextAlign = \"MiddleCenter\" Flag combinations use the .NET text form: \"Top, Left\" ."}, {"u": "concepts.html#value-types-and-dnnull", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Value types and dnNull()", "x": "Value types and dnNull() # Structs ( Point , Size , Color , DateTime ) are objects like any other. Their constructors take the numbers: new Point(10,20) , new DateTime(2024,12,25) : oButton.Location = new Point(10,20) oButton.Size = new Size(100,30) oPictureBox.Image = dnNull() # the .NET null dnNull() is the .NET null . A .NET method that returns null gives an object whose isNull() is true."}, {"u": "concepts.html#out-and-ref-parameters", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "out and ref parameters", "x": "out and ref parameters # A method with out or ref parameters returns a list : [result, out values...] : aResult = oDict.TryGetValue(\"key\") # [1, value] or [0, \"\"] if aResult[1] ? aResult[2] ok aParsed = new Int32().TryParse(\"123\") # [1, 123]"}, {"u": "concepts.html#generics", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Generics", "x": "Generics # The most useful closed generic types have classes: StringList , NumberList , ObjectList ( List<T> ), StringDictionary , NumberDictionary , ObjectDictionary ( Dictionary<string,T> ), StringSet , StringQueue , StringStack . See Collections & LINQ . Any other closed generic type can be created without a class: oList = dnNewGeneric(\"System.Collections.Generic.List\",[\"System.Int32\"],[]) oList.callMethod(\"Add\",[5]) Generic methods work too (the bridge infers the type arguments from the values), which is what makes LINQ possible."}, {"u": "concepts.html#return-values", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Return values", "x": "Return values # .NET returns Ring gets string string numbers ( int , long , double , decimal ...) number bool 1 / 0 enum string byte[] string (binary safe) arrays and collections of simple values list (use toList() on a collection object) any other object a wrapped object (generated class, or a plain DotNetObject ) null an object whose isNull() is true"}, {"u": "concepts.html#errors", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Errors", "x": "Errors # A .NET exception becomes a Ring error and can be caught with try / catch : try new Int32().Parse(\"abc\") catch ? cCatchError done DotNet Error: System.FormatException: The input string 'abc' was not in a correct format. Set the environment variable RINGDOTNET_TRACE=1 to get the .NET stack trace in the message."}, {"u": "concepts.html#plain-objects", "t": "Core concepts", "h": "Plain objects", "x": "Plain objects # When no class exists for a type, or when you want the generic style, every wrapped object is also a DotNetObject and has: Method What it does callMethod(name, [args]) call a method getProp(name) / setProp(name, value) read or write a property or field toString() ToString() of the object typeName() full .NET type name toList() copy an array or collection into a Ring list isNull() true for the .NET null release() release the handle (rarely needed)"}, {"u": "core-api.html", "t": "Core functions", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "core-api.html#runtime", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Runtime", "x": "Runtime # dnVersion() # Returns the version of the .NET runtime hosted in the process. load \"dotnetlib.ring\" ? dnVersion() # .NET 9.0.15 dnUseDesktop(), dnUseWeb(), dnUseAll(), dnUseData() # Select the runtime mode before the first .NET call. See Getting started ."}, {"u": "core-api.html#importing-classes", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Importing classes", "x": "Importing classes # Function What it does dnImport(\"System.IO\") Loads the generated wrapper classes of a namespace (once). Returns true. Raises an error when no classes were generated for the name. using { \"System\" \"System.Text\" } The same as dnImport for each text, written like C#'s using . A value that is not a text raises an error. dnImportFrom(\"myclasses/\",\"MyCompany.Tools\") Loads classes generated for your own assembly with wrapassembly.ring . See Your own assemblies . dnRegisterClass(type, class, nInit) Used by the generated files: registers a Ring class for a .NET type. dnImport(\"System"}, {"u": "core-api.html#loading-assemblies", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Loading assemblies", "x": "Loading assemblies # Function What it does dnLoad(\"path/to/Library.dll\") Loads an assembly from a path and returns its name. Its dependencies are found next to it. dnLoadName(\"System.Xml\") Loads an assembly by name."}, {"u": "core-api.html#without-wrapper-classes", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Without wrapper classes", "x": "Without wrapper classes # Any type is usable without generated classes: # a type, for static members oConsole = dnType(\"System.Console\") oConsole.callMethod(\"WriteLine\",[\"Hello from .NET\"]) # a static call in one line ? dnStatic(\"System.Math\",\"Max\",[3,9]) # 9 # a new object oSB = dnNew(\"System.Text.StringBuilder\",[\"Hello\"]) oSB.callMethod(\"Append\",[\", Ring!\"]) ? oSB.toString() # Hello, Ring! ? oSB.getProp(\"Length\") # 12 oSB.setProp(\"Capacity\",64) Function Returns dnType(\"Namespace.Type\") a type object: use callMethod / getProp for static members dnNew(\"Namespace.Type\", [args]) a new object (th"}, {"u": "core-api.html#dotnetobject", "t": "Core functions", "h": "DotNetObject", "x": "DotNetObject # Every .NET object that Ring holds is a DotNetObject (the generated classes derive from it): oObject.callMethod(\"Method\",[arg1,arg2]) # call a method oObject.getProp(\"Name\") # read a property or field oObject.setProp(\"Name\",value) # write it oObject.toString() # ToString() oObject.typeName() # \"System.Text.StringBuilder\" oObject.toList() # an array or a collection as a Ring list oObject.isNull() # true for the .NET null oObject.release() # release the handle oObject.nHandle is the handle number."}, {"u": "core-api.html#dnrequest", "t": "Core functions", "h": "dnRequest", "x": "dnRequest # dnRequest(operation, params) sends one request to the bridge; everything above is built on it. The operations are type new call get set tostring typename tolist describe load loadname release version . You normally never need it."}, {"u": "core-api.html#callbacks", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Callbacks", "x": "Callbacks # dnCallback(code) passes Ring code where a delegate is expected. See Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "core-api.html#test-helpers", "t": "Core functions", "h": "Test helpers", "x": "Test helpers # dnAssert(\"name\", actual, expected) # counts a pass or prints FAIL: name expected <..> got <..> dnTestSummary() # prints \"TESTS: n passed, m failed\" nDotNetPass and nDotNetFail hold the counters. See Testing ."}, {"u": "collections-linq.html", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#typed-collections", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Typed collections", "x": "Typed collections # The common closed generic collections have ready-made classes ( dnImport(\"System.Collections.Generic\") ): Class .NET type StringList , NumberList , ObjectList List<string> , List<double> , List<object> StringDictionary , NumberDictionary , ObjectDictionary Dictionary<string, T> StringSet HashSet<string> StringQueue , StringStack Queue<string> , Stack<string> dnImport(\"System.Collections.Generic\") oList = new StringList() oList.Add(\"banana\") oList.Add(\"apple\") oList.Add(\"cherry\") oList.Sort() ? oList.Count for cItem in oList.toList() # toList() copies the collection to a Rin"}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#other-collection-classes", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Other collection classes", "x": "Other collection classes # System.Collections , System.Collections.Specialized , System.Collections.Concurrent and System.Collections.ObjectModel are wrapped completely: ArrayList , Hashtable , SortedList , Stack , Queue , BitArray , NameValueCollection , ObservableCollection and so on. samples/UsingDotNet/test10_collections.ring load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnImport(\"System.Collections\") dnImport(\"System.Collections.Specialized\") dnImport(\"System.Collections.Generic\") # Word frequency with Dictionary<string,double> (entries are wrapped KeyValuePair objects) oCount = new Num"}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#sorting-and-searching-with-ring-code", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "Sorting and searching with Ring code", "x": "Sorting and searching with Ring code # Methods that take a delegate ( Predicate , Comparison , Action ) accept dnCallback(...) . The Ring code reads aDotNetArgs and sets xDotNetResult : oList.FindAll(dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = left(aDotNetArgs[1],1) = 'a'\")) oList.Sort_1(dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = len(aDotNetArgs[1]) - len(aDotNetArgs[2])\")) oList.ForEach_(dnCallback(\"? aDotNetArgs[1]\")) See Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "collections-linq.html#linq", "t": "Collections & LINQ", "h": "LINQ", "x": "LINQ # dnImport(\"System.Linq\") loads Enumerable and a fluent wrapper, dnQuery(...) , that works on a Ring list or any .NET collection . The lambda is Ring code: it reads aDotNetArgs[1] and writes xDotNetResult . dnImport(\"System.Linq\") aSales = [120, 45, 300, 15, 210, 99, 81] aTop = dnQuery(aSales).Where(\"xDotNetResult = aDotNetArgs[1] >= 100\").OrderByDescending(\"xDotNetResult = aDotNetArgs[1]\").Take(3).ToList() ? dnQuery(aSales).Count() ? dnQuery(aSales).Sum(\"\") ? dnQuery(aSales).Average(\"\") ? dnQuery(aSales).Max(\"\") samples/UsingDotNet/test11_linq.ring load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetl"}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#callbacks-with-dncallback", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Callbacks with dnCallback()", "x": "Callbacks with dnCallback() # Pass dnCallback(code) wherever a .NET delegate is expected: Action , Func , Predicate , Comparison , EventHandler , Task continuations... The bridge builds a delegate of the exact type . When .NET invokes it, the Ring code runs, as with events in RingQt. It can read aDotNetArgs (the list of the delegate arguments) and set xDotNetResult (converted to the delegate's return type). nTotal = 0 oList.ForEach_(dnCallback(\"nTotal += len(aDotNetArgs[1])\")) # ForEach is a Ring keyword: ForEach_ oList.Sort_1(dnCallback(\"xDotNetResult = len(aDotNetArgs[1]) - len(aDotNetArgs[2"}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#events", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # Every public .NET event gets two methods, as in RingQt: setXXXEvent(code) and clearXXXEvent() . The code runs when the event is raised and can read aDotNetArgs , which is [sender, eventArgs] (both .NET objects). Each event has one handler; setting it again replaces it. dnImport(\"System.Collections.ObjectModel\") oList = new ObservableStringList() oList.setCollectionChangedEvent(\"? 'changed: ' + aDotNetArgs[2].getProp('Action')\") oList.Add(\"one\") CollectionChanged event #1 CollectionChanged event #2 CollectionChanged event #3 Events received : 3 Items : 1 (second handler) Items : 3 acti"}, {"u": "callbacks-events.html#calling-ring-from-net-code-you-wrote", "t": "Callbacks & events", "h": "Calling Ring from .NET code you wrote", "x": "Calling Ring from .NET code you wrote # Your own assembly can call Ring as well: when a parameter is typed object , the bridge passes the callback unchanged and your code can call RingDotNet.Bridge.CallRing(callback, returnType, args) . This is how the Entity Framework hooks ( onSaving , onLog ) and value converters are built \u2014 see Entity Framework reference ."}, {"u": "foundation.html", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "foundation.html#what-is-wrapped", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "What is wrapped", "x": "What is wrapped # Every namespace below has generated classes, loaded on demand with dnImport(\"Name\") . Area Namespaces Core System , System.Globalization , System.Numerics , System.Reflection , System.Runtime.InteropServices , System.Runtime.Loader , System.ComponentModel , System.Resources Text System.Text , System.Text.RegularExpressions , System.Text.Json , System.Text.Json.Nodes , System.Text.Encodings.Web , System.Text.Unicode Files and streams System.IO , System.IO.Compression , System.IO.Pipes , System.IO.MemoryMappedFiles , System.Formats.Tar Collections System.Collections , .Generic "}, {"u": "foundation.html#dates-strings-conversions-math", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Dates, strings, conversions, math", "x": "Dates, strings, conversions, math # DateTime , TimeSpan , Convert , Math , String helpers, Guid , Random , Environment : samples/UsingDotNet/test08_system.ring load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnImport(\"System\") # Dates and times oNow = new DateTime(2024,12,25).AddHours(18.5) ? \"Christmas 2024 : \" + oNow.ToString_1(\"dddd, dd MMMM yyyy HH:mm\") ? \"Plus 10 days : \" + oNow.AddDays(10).ToString_1(\"yyyy-MM-dd\") ? \"Day of week : \" + oNow.DayOfWeek oSpan = new TimeSpan().FromMinutes(135) ? \"135 minutes : \" + oSpan.ToString() + \" (\" + oSpan.TotalHours + \" hours)\" # Strings, conversions, "}, {"u": "foundation.html#files-regular-expressions-and-compression", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Files, regular expressions and compression", "x": "Files, regular expressions and compression # File , Directory , Path , StreamReader / StreamWriter , MemoryStream , Regex , GZipStream : samples/UsingDotNet/test09_io_regex.ring load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnImport(\"System\") dnImport(\"System.IO\") dnImport(\"System.IO.Compression\") dnImport(\"System.Text.RegularExpressions\") oPath = new Path() oFile = new File() cDir = oPath.Combine(oPath.GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_demo\") new Directory().CreateDirectory(cDir) # Text files cLog = oPath.Combine(cDir,\"log.txt\") oWriter = new StreamWriter(cLog) oWriter.WriteLine(\"2024-05-17 ERROR d"}, {"u": "foundation.html#networking", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Networking", "x": "Networking # Uri , IPAddress , Dns , HttpClient , WebUtility , Encoding \u2014 a program that does not need the internet to run: samples/UsingDotNet/test12_network.ring load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnImport(\"System\") dnImport(\"System.Text\") dnImport(\"System.Net\") dnImport(\"System.Net.Http\") dnImport(\"System.Threading.Tasks\") # A tiny web server and client in one Ring program: .NET runs the requests on its thread pool, # Ring waits for the tasks and answers cPrefix = \"http://localhost:\" + (20000 + random(20000)) + \"/\" oServer = new HttpListener() oServer.Prefixes.Add(cPrefix) oSer"}, {"u": "foundation.html#cryptography-and-globalization", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Cryptography and globalization", "x": "Cryptography and globalization # Hashes ( SHA256 , MD5 ), HMAC, random bytes, AES, culture-aware formatting: samples/UsingDotNet/test13_security.ring load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnImport(\"System\") dnImport(\"System.Globalization\") dnImport(\"System.Security.Cryptography\") dnImport(\"System.Diagnostics\") # Hashes and HMAC oConvert = new Convert() ? \"SHA256(abc) : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new SHA256().HashData(\"abc\"))) ? \"MD5(abc) : \" + lower(oConvert.ToHexString(new MD5().HashData(\"abc\"))) oHmac = new HMACSHA256().New_1(\"secret key\") ? \"HMAC : \" + left(lower(oConvert.ToHe"}, {"u": "foundation.html#xml-json-and-data-tables", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "XML, JSON and data tables", "x": "XML, JSON and data tables # XmlDocument , XPath , System.Text.Json , DataTable and DataView \u2014 no database needed: samples/UsingDotNet/test14_xml_json_data.ring load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnImport(\"System.Xml\") dnImport(\"System.Text.Json\") dnImport(\"System.Data\") # XML with XPath oDoc = new XmlDocument() oDoc.LoadXml(\"<shop><item price='3.5'>apple</item><item price='1.2'>fig</item><item price='9'>melon</item></shop>\") oItems = oDoc.SelectNodes(\"//item[@price > 2]\") ? \"Items over 2 : \" + oItems.Count for nLoop = 0 to oItems.Count - 1 oItem = oItems.Item(nLoop) ? \" \" + oItem."}, {"u": "foundation.html#reflection-and-big-numbers", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Reflection and big numbers", "x": "Reflection and big numbers # Type , Assembly , MethodInfo , BigInteger , Complex : samples/UsingDotNet/test15_reflection_numbers.ring load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnImport(\"System\") dnImport(\"System.Reflection\") dnImport(\"System.Numerics\") dnImport(\"System.Text.Encodings.Web\") dnImport(\"System.Collections.Concurrent\") dnImport(\"System.Runtime.InteropServices\") # Big integers: factorial of 40 oBig = new BigInteger() nFactorial = oBig.Parse(\"1\") for nLoop = 2 to 40 nFactorial = oBig.Multiply(nFactorial,nLoop) next ? \"40! : \" + nFactorial.ToString() ? \"2^200 : \" + oBig.Pow(2,20"}, {"u": "foundation.html#processes-and-threads", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "Processes and threads", "x": "Processes and threads # dnImport(\"System.Diagnostics\") dnImport(\"System.Threading.Tasks\") oInfo = new ProcessStartInfo() oInfo.FileName = \"cmd\" oInfo.Arguments = \"/c ver\" oInfo.RedirectStandardOutput = 1 oInfo.UseShellExecute = 0 oInfo.CreateNoWindow = 1 oProcess = new Process().Start_1(oInfo) ? trim(oProcess.StandardOutput.ReadToEnd()) oProcess.WaitForExit() oTask = new Task().Delay(30) # a Task: wait for it on the Ring thread oTask.Wait() ? oTask.IsCompleted oResult = new Task().FromResult(\"done\") ? oResult.getProp(\"Result\") # the Result of a Task<T> Warning .NET threads can not run Ring cod"}, {"u": "foundation.html#a-bigger-example", "t": "The .NET class library", "h": "A bigger example", "x": "A bigger example # samples/UsingDotNet/test16_forms_app.ring and demo_todo.ring combine collections, files, JSON and a form. For complete programs see Applications ."}, {"u": "winforms.html", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "winforms.html#the-shape-of-a-program", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "The shape of a program", "x": "The shape of a program # load \"dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseDesktop() # the Windows Desktop runtime, and an STA Ring thread dnImport(\"System.Drawing\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Forms\") # also loads the GUI helpers oForm = \"\" # globals used by event code oCount = \"\" nClicks = 0 func main oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) oCount = uiLabel(oForm,\"0 clicks\",20,15,220,24) uiButton(oForm,\"Click me\",20,50,100,32,\"clicked()\") uiRun(oForm) func clicked nClicks++ oCount.Text = \"\" + nClicks + \" clicks\" A label and a button: the whole program is about 15 lines dnUseDesktop() first, then the imports, then the"}, {"u": "winforms.html#the-helpers", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "The helpers", "x": "The helpers # Everything the helpers do can also be written with the plain classes (see the next section); they exist to keep small programs short. They are loaded together with System.Windows.Forms . Helper What it creates uiForm(title, w, h) a form with a client area of w \u00d7 h, centered uiLabel(parent, text, x, y, w, h) a label uiButton(parent, text, x, y, w, h, clickCode) a button; clickCode is Ring code or a function call uiTextBox(parent, x, y, w, h) a text box uiMemo(parent, x, y, w, h) a multi-line text box with scroll bars uiCheckBox(parent, text, x, y, w, h, clickCode) a check box uiLi"}, {"u": "winforms.html#every-control-is-available", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Every control is available", "x": "Every control is available # The helpers are optional. The whole of System.Windows.Forms (414 classes), System.Drawing and its sub-namespaces are wrapped, so you can use any control directly: oTree = new TreeView() oTree.Dock = \"Fill\" oRoot = oTree.Nodes.Add(\"Fruit\") oRoot.Nodes.Add(\"Apple\") oRoot.Nodes.Add(\"Pear\") oTree.ExpandAll() oForm.Controls.Add(oTree) Group Controls Text and input Label , TextBox , RichTextBox , MaskedTextBox , NumericUpDown , DateTimePicker , MonthCalendar Buttons and choices Button , CheckBox , RadioButton , ComboBox , ListBox , CheckedListBox , TrackBar , ProgressBar"}, {"u": "winforms.html#events", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # oButton.setClickEvent(\"clicked()\") oText.setTextChangedEvent(\"textChanged()\") oForm.setFormClosingEvent(\"closing()\") oPicture.setMouseDownEvent(\"? aDotNetArgs[2].X\") The code of an event runs on the Ring thread when the message loop dispatches it. Details and warnings: Callbacks & events ."}, {"u": "winforms.html#keyboard-in-games", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Keyboard in games", "x": "Keyboard in games # A button (or any control that takes the focus) steals the arrow keys, Space and Enter from the form, so the form's KeyDown never sees them. uiGameKeys fixes that: oForm.setKeyDownEvent(\"keyPressed(aDotNetArgs[2].KeyCode)\") uiGameKeys(oForm,\"keyPressed(aDotNetArgs[2].KeyCode)\") # after the controls were added Every control then reports the arrows as normal keys and the form handles them. Tetris, 2048, Sokoban, Snake and Pong use it. applications/Tetris \u2014 keys are handled by the form"}, {"u": "winforms.html#self-testing-programs", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "Self-testing programs", "x": "Self-testing programs # Every sample and application can drive itself and check its results \u2014 the same program, no separate test harness: uiTest(oForm,\"selfTest()\") # when started with --selftest: run selfTest() once the form is shown uiRun(oForm) uiEnd() # prints SELFTEST OK, or the failures func selfTest oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() dnAssert(\"one click\",oCount.Text,\"1 clicks\") uiSendKeys(oForm,\"12{+}30{ENTER}\") types into the focused control through the real key processing by posting window messages to your own form \u2014 it can never type into another program. See Testing ."}, {"u": "winforms.html#more", "t": "Windows Forms", "h": "More", "x": "More # Forms samples \u2014 32 short samples. Applications \u2014 43 complete programs. WPF \u2014 the other desktop UI framework."}, {"u": "gallery-forms.html", "t": "Windows Forms samples", "h": "", "x": "Run one with ring 06_listbox.ring from samples/Forms ; with --selftest it checks itself and closes. Click Source code on a card to read the program, or click the picture to enlarge it. Hello form The smallest window: a form with a label ring samples/Forms/01_hello_form.ring Source code # The smallest window: a form with a label load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseDesktop() dnImport(\"System.Drawing\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Forms\") oForm = uiForm(\"Hello\",300,120) oLabel = uiLabel(oForm,\"Hello from Ring and .NET!\",20,40,260,30) oLabel.Font = uiFont(\"Segoe UI\",14,true) uiTest(oF"}, {"u": "gallery-apps.html", "t": "Applications", "h": "", "x": "Run one: cd applications/Calculator then ring main.ring . With ring main.ring --selftest the application drives its own form, checks the results with dnAssert() and prints SELFTEST OK . Ports of RingQt applications: Calculator, Stopwatch, TicTacToe, FifteenPuzzle, LightsOut, Game2048, MemoryGame, Snake, TowersOfHanoi, AnalogClock, RockPaperScissors, BMICalculator, GameOfLife, Minesweeper, EpochTime, AsciiTable, Calendar, EightQueens, Sudoku, Pong, Tetris, Sokoban, TypingQuiz, JulianDay. The others are new: Notepad, Paint, TodoList, ColorMixer, NumberGuess, Hangman, DiceRoller, UnitConverter, P"}, {"u": "wpf.html", "t": "WPF", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "wpf.html#a-first-wpf-window", "t": "WPF", "h": "A first WPF window", "x": "A first WPF window # load \"dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseDesktop() dnImport(\"System.Windows\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Controls\") oWindow = wpfWindow(\"Hello\",300,200) oPanel = new StackPanel() oWindow.Content = oPanel oPanel.Children.Add(wpfLabel(\"Hello from WPF\")) wpfRun(oWindow) dnUseDesktop() starts the desktop runtime. Then import the WPF namespaces you need: System.Windows , .Controls , .Controls.Primitives , .Media , .Shapes , .Data , .Input , .Documents , .Markup , .Threading , .Media.Imaging , .Media.Animation ..."}, {"u": "wpf.html#names-that-start-with-wpf", "t": "WPF", "h": "Names that start with Wpf", "x": "Names that start with Wpf # WPF has classes with the same names as Windows Forms and System.Drawing classes. To keep both usable in one program, the WPF version of a clashing name starts with Wpf : Windows Forms / System.Drawing WPF Button , TextBox , Label WpfButton , WpfTextBox , WpfLabel Point , Color , Brush WpfPoint , WpfColor , WpfBrush Binding , Rectangle WpfBinding , WpfRectangle The others keep their name: Window , Grid , StackPanel , Canvas , Slider , Ellipse , Thickness ... Warning A global variable named like an attribute ( x , width ) breaks obj.X in Ring. Keep program state in fu"}, {"u": "wpf.html#the-helpers", "t": "WPF", "h": "The helpers", "x": "The helpers # Helper Does wpfWindow(title, w, h) a window of the given size, centered wpfLabel(text) , wpfText(text) a Label / TextBlock wpfButton(text, clickCode) a button with a click handler wpfBrush(\"SteelBlue\") , wpfColor(name) a brush or color from a name or #RRGGBB wpfXaml(text) the objects of a XAML text wpfFind(root, name) an element by its x:Name wpfRun(window) shows the window and runs the message loop wpfTest(window, code) , wpfEnd() self test ( --selftest ) wpfPixel(element, w, h, x, y) the color of a pixel of a rendered element (tests)"}, {"u": "wpf.html#xaml", "t": "WPF", "h": "XAML", "x": "XAML # Describe the window in XAML and find the elements by name: cXaml = '<Window xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" xmlns:x=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml\" Title=\"From XAML\" Width=\"320\" Height=\"180\">' cXaml += '<StackPanel Margin=\"15\"><TextBlock x:Name=\"message\" FontSize=\"18\" Text=\"Press the button\"/>' cXaml += '<Button x:Name=\"press\" Content=\"Press me\" Margin=\"0,10,0,0\"/></StackPanel></Window>' oWindow = wpfXaml(cXaml) oMessage = wpfFind(oWindow,\"message\") oPress = wpfFind(oWindow,\"press\") oPress.setClickEvent(\"oMessage.Text = 'The XAML button w"}, {"u": "wpf.html#data-binding", "t": "WPF", "h": "Data binding", "x": "Data binding # Bind a property of one element to a property of another \u2014 without code: oSlider = new Slider() oSlider.Maximum = 100 oText = new WpfTextBox() oBinding = new WpfBinding().New_1(\"Value\") oBinding.Source = oSlider oText.SetBinding(oText.TextProperty,oBinding) samples/Wpf/03_binding_slider.ring \u2014 the text follows the slider"}, {"u": "wpf.html#shapes-and-brushes", "t": "WPF", "h": "Shapes and brushes", "x": "Shapes and brushes # oCanvas = new Canvas() oRect = new WpfRectangle() oRect.Width = 80 oRect.Height = 50 oRect.Fill = wpfBrush(\"SteelBlue\") oCanvas.Children.Add(oRect) oCanvas.SetLeft(oRect,10) oCanvas.SetTop(oRect,10) samples/Wpf/07_shapes_pixels.ring"}, {"u": "wpf.html#layout", "t": "WPF", "h": "Layout", "x": "Layout # Grid , StackPanel , DockPanel , WrapPanel , Canvas , UniformGrid and the Thickness margin work as in XAML: samples/Wpf/08_grid_layout.ring"}, {"u": "wpf.html#events", "t": "WPF", "h": "Events", "x": "Events # Events work like Windows Forms events: setClickEvent , setValueChangedEvent , setTextChangedEvent ... Routed events can be raised from tests with RaiseEvent ."}, {"u": "wpf.html#applications", "t": "WPF", "h": "Applications", "x": "Applications # applications/WpfCalculator \u2014 a calculator whose UI is XAML. applications/WpfColorMixer \u2014 sliders that mix a color. applications/WpfCalculator More: WPF samples \u2014 all 10 with screenshots and source."}, {"u": "gallery-wpf.html", "t": "WPF samples", "h": "", "x": "Run one with ring 06_xaml_window.ring from samples/Wpf ; with --selftest it checks itself and closes. See the WPF guide for the concepts. Hello window The smallest WPF window ring samples/Wpf/01_hello_window.ring Source code # The smallest WPF window load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseDesktop() dnImport(\"System.Windows\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Controls\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Controls.Primitives\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Media\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Shapes\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Data\") dnImport(\"System.Windows.Threading\") dnImport(\"System\") oWindow = wpfWindow(\""}, {"u": "aspnet.html", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "aspnet.html#a-web-server-in-a-few-lines", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "A web server in a few lines", "x": "A web server in a few lines # load \"dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseWeb() # before any other .NET call oServer = new WebServer(\"http://localhost:5080\") oServer.onGet(\"/\",\"home\") oServer.onPost(\"/api/echo\",\"echo\") oServer.run() # serves until stop() func home oRequest return \"<h1>Hello</h1>\" + oRequest.path() func echo oRequest return [201,\"text/plain\",\"you sent \" + oRequest.body()] dnUseWeb() starts the ASP.NET Core runtime and loads the web server of RingDotNet. A handler is a Ring function that receives the request and returns the response. applications/WebApi \u2014 a page rendered by a Ring handler"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#handlers-and-responses", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "Handlers and responses", "x": "Handlers and responses # A handler returns one of: Return value Response a text status 200, text/html [status, contentType, text] exactly that webJson(value) status 200, application/json webJson turns a Ring list into a JSON array and a list of [name, value] pairs into a JSON object (nested values too): func listTodos oRequest aList = [] for aTodo in aTodos aList + [[\"id\",aTodo[1]],[\"text\",aTodo[2]]] next return webJson(aList) GET /api/todos"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#the-request", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "The request", "x": "The request # Method Returns oRequest.method() \"GET\" , \"POST\" ... oRequest.path() /api/todos oRequest.queryString() ?id=1 oRequest.query(\"id\") one query value oRequest.body() the body as text oRequest.header(\"Accept\") a request header oRequest.form(\"name\") a form field of a POST oRequest.setHeader(name, value) add a response header oRequest.context() the ASP.NET HttpContext , for everything else"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#the-webserver", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "The WebServer", "x": "The WebServer # Method Does new WebServer(url) starts Kestrel; port 0 picks a free port onGet(path, handler) , onPost , onPut , onDelete routes ( route(method, path, handler) for any method) notFound(handler) the handler for unknown paths (default: Not found: GET /x ) url() the real address (when the port was 0) run() serve until stop() serveOne(milliseconds) handle one waiting request; true if one was handled stop() , close() stop serving, stop the server An error raised by a handler becomes a 500 response with the message. nRequests counts the requests served."}, {"u": "aspnet.html#one-ring-thread", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "One Ring thread", "x": "One Ring thread # Kestrel runs on .NET threads and Ring code may only run on the Ring thread. So the requests are queued and serveOne() / run() take them off the queue on the Ring thread. This is the reason you can use any Ring code and global state in a handler without locks \u2014 and also why handlers do not run in parallel. You can mix serving with your own loop (for example next to a GUI, using dnUseAll() ): while true oServer.serveOne(100) # handle a request if one arrives within 100 ms doOtherWork() end"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#a-complete-application", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "A complete application", "x": "A complete application # applications/WebApi is a to-do list with a web page and a JSON API. It tests itself by calling its own server with HttpClient : applications/WebApi/main.ring # A small web application written in Ring on ASP.NET Core: a to-do list with a web page and a JSON API # ring main.ring then open http://localhost:5080/ # GET /api/todos POST /api/todos (the text is the body) DELETE /api/todos?id=1 load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseWeb() dnImport(\"System\") dnImport(\"System.Net.Http\") dnImport(\"System.Threading.Tasks\") aTodos = [] # [id, text] nNextId = 1 oServer"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#all-of-aspnet-core", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "All of ASP.NET Core", "x": "All of ASP.NET Core # The ASP.NET Core classes are wrapped too: Microsoft.AspNetCore.* (Http, Routing, Authentication, Authorization, Mvc, Builder, Hosting, StaticFiles...), Microsoft.Extensions.* (DependencyInjection, Configuration, Logging, Hosting, Caching...) and Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers . Class names that clash with other namespaces start with Asp . dnImport(\"Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http\") oPath = new PathString().New_1(\"/api/items\") ? oPath.Value # /api/items ? oPath.StartsWithSegments(new PathString().New_1(\"/api\")) oQuery = new QueryString().Create_2(\"name\",\"ring dotnet\") ? oQuery.Value"}, {"u": "aspnet.html#what-is-not-supported", "t": "ASP.NET Core", "h": "What is not supported", "x": "What is not supported # MVC controllers and Razor pages written in C# \u2014 the web server is the Ring one. Handlers that run in parallel (there is one Ring thread). Calling Ring from middleware you wrote in .NET."}, {"u": "ef.html", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "ef.html#the-idea", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "The idea", "x": "The idea # Ring cannot declare .NET classes, so a Ring program describes the model with data : a list of tables, each with a list of columns. EF Core works on that model in two ways: Class Rows are Best for RingDatabase lists of [name, value] pairs scripts, tools, small apps \u2014 the simplest API RingEntityDb real .NET objects (classes generated from the schema) with navigation properties change tracking, Include , an object-oriented style Both support SQLite (bundled), an in-memory database, and SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL when their EF Core package is available. load \"dotnetlib.ring\" dnUse"}, {"u": "ef.html#a-table-and-its-rows", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "A table and its rows", "x": "A table and its rows # The schema is a list of tables: [tableName, [ [column, type, isKey, options], ... ]] . Column types: int long double decimal string bool datetime bytes . An int or long key is generated by the database. docs/examples/ef_basic.ring # Entity Framework Core: a table, rows, SQL conditions load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseData() func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Customers\",[ [\"Id\",\"int\",1], [\"Name\",\"string\",0,[[\"required\",1],[\"maxlength\",40]]], [\"Age\",\"int\",0,[[\"nullable\",1]]], [\"City\",\"string\",0,[[\"index\",1]]] ]] ]) nAnn = oDb.in"}, {"u": "ef.html#column-options", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Column options", "x": "Column options # The optional fourth item of a column is a list of [name, value] options: Option Meaning [\"required\",1] NOT NULL for text columns [\"nullable\",1] the column accepts null (a number can then be empty) [\"unique\",1] a unique index \u2014 a duplicate raises an error [\"index\",1] a plain index [\"maxlength\",40] maximum length of a text [\"default\",5] default value (numbers or text) [\"references\",\"Authors\"] foreign key to the key of another table [\"ondelete\",\"cascade\"] cascade , restrict , setnull , noaction [\"convert\",\"upper\"] a value converter (see hooks ) Several columns with isKey = 1 make"}, {"u": "ef.html#relationships", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Relationships", "x": "Relationships # A column with [\"references\",\"Authors\"] becomes a foreign key. children , parent and include read the related rows: docs/examples/ef_relations.ring # Entity Framework Core: foreign keys, children, parent, include and delete behaviors load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseData() func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Authors\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0]]], [\"Books\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Title\",\"string\",0],[\"AuthorId\",\"int\",0,[[\"references\",\"Authors\"],[\"ondelete\",\"cascade\"]]]]] ]) nTolkien = oDb.insert(\"Authors\",[[\"Name\",\"Tolkien\"]]) nLewis "}, {"u": "ef.html#queries-as-data", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Queries as data", "x": "Queries as data # For anything beyond a SQL condition, describe the query as data. EF Core builds a real LINQ expression tree from it and translates it to SQL, so it works with every provider: docs/examples/ef_queries.ring # Entity Framework Core: queries as LINQ expression trees, aggregates and SQL of any shape load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseData() func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"People\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0],[\"Age\",\"int\",0],[\"City\",\"string\",0],[\"Score\",\"double\",0]]] ]) oDb.insert(\"People\",[[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"Age\",31],[\"City\",\"Cair"}, {"u": "ef.html#transactions-and-manual-saving", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Transactions and manual saving", "x": "Transactions and manual saving # oDb.begin() oDb.update(\"Accounts\",nA,[[\"Balance\",70]]) oDb.update(\"Accounts\",nB,[[\"Balance\",80]]) oDb.commit() # or oDb.rollback() oDb.autoSave(false) # changes are written together by save() oDb.insert(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"D\"],[\"Balance\",5]]) oDb.insert(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"E\"],[\"Balance\",6]]) oDb.save() oDb.autoSave(true) The async versions return a .NET Task ; dbWait waits for it and returns the result: oTask = oDb.insertAsync(\"Accounts\",[[\"Owner\",\"F\"],[\"Balance\",7]]) ? dbWait(oTask) # the key ? len(dbWait(oDb.allAsync(\"Accounts\")))"}, {"u": "ef.html#migrations", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Migrations", "x": "Migrations # migrate() brings an existing database in line with the model: it creates the missing tables, columns and indexes and returns the list of changes. apply(name, sql) runs a named SQL change once and records it in the table RingMigrations . docs/examples/ef_migrate.ring # Entity Framework Core: migrations of an existing database file load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseData() dnImport(\"System.IO\") func main cFile = new Path().Combine(new Path().GetTempPath(),\"ringdotnet_docs_migrate.db\") if new File().Exists(cFile) new File().Delete(cFile) ok cConnection = \"Data Sourc"}, {"u": "ef.html#hooks-filters-and-converters", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Hooks, filters and converters", "x": "Hooks, filters and converters # Ring functions can run when changes are saved or SQL is executed; a table can have a global query filter (soft delete); a column can have a value converter : docs/examples/ef_hooks.ring # Entity Framework Core: hooks, query filters, converters, transactions load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseData() aSql = [] func main oDb = new RingDatabase(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Notes\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Text\",\"string\",0,[[\"convert\",\"upper\"]]],[\"Deleted\",\"int\",0]],[[\"filter\",[\"Deleted\",\"=\",0]]]] ]) oDb.onSaving(dnCallback(\"beforeSave\")) oDb.onLog(dnCallback"}, {"u": "ef.html#real-entity-classes", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Real entity classes", "x": "Real entity classes # RingEntityDb uses the same schema, but each table becomes a real .NET class with one property per column, a navigation property to the parent for each foreign key ( AuthorId \u2192 Author ) and a collection on the parent ( Books ). EF tracks the objects like it tracks classes written in C#: docs/examples/ef_entities.ring # Entity Framework Core with real entity classes: navigation properties, tracking, include load \"../../libraries/dotnetlib/dotnetlib.ring\" dnUseData() func main oDb = new RingEntityDb(\"Data Source=:memory:\",[ [\"Authors\",[[\"Id\",\"int\",1],[\"Name\",\"string\",0]]], ["}, {"u": "ef.html#providers", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "Providers", "x": "Providers # The connection text selects the provider: Connection Provider \"Data Source=people.db\" SQLite (bundled). \"Data Source=:memory:\" for an in-memory SQLite database \"inmemory:name\" the EF in-memory provider (no SQL, no migrations; same name = same database) \"sqlserver:Server=.;Database=x;...\" SQL Server \u2014 needs Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer.dll next to the other assemblies \"postgres:Host=...;...\" PostgreSQL (Npgsql provider) \"mysql:Server=...;...\" MySQL (Pomelo provider) Warning The SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL providers are loaded by name from their NuGet packages and hav"}, {"u": "ef.html#the-full-ef-api", "t": "Entity Framework Core", "h": "The full EF API", "x": "The full EF API # Everything in EF Core is also wrapped as classes ( dnImport(\"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore\") , .ChangeTracking , .Infrastructure , .Metadata , .Metadata.Builders , .Storage , .Query , .Migrations , .Diagnostics , Microsoft.Data.Sqlite ; clashing names start with Ef ). oDb.context() returns the real DbContext : oContext = oDb.context() ? oContext.Database.ProviderName # Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite The complete list of methods is in the Entity Framework reference . A complete program: applications/Contacts \u2014 a contacts book with a console menu."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#schema-format", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Schema format", "x": "Schema format # aSchema = [ [ \"TableName\", [ [ \"Column\", \"type\", isKey, [ [\"option\", value], ... ] ], ... ], [ [\"tableOption\", value], ... ] # optional ], ... ] Part Values column type int , long , double , decimal , string (or text ), bool , datetime , bytes isKey 1 for a key column; several give a composite key. A single int / long key is generated by the database column options required , nullable , unique , index , maxlength , default , references , ondelete , convert table options [\"filter\", condition] \u2014 a global query filter"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#creating-a-database", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Creating a database", "x": "Creating a database # oDb = new RingDatabase(connection, aSchema) # rows as [name, value] pairs oDb = new RingEntityDb(connection, aSchema) # real entity objects The connection starts with the provider name ( inmemory: , sqlserver: , postgres: , mysql: ) or is a SQLite connection string."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#ringdatabase", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "RingDatabase", "x": "RingDatabase # Rows # Method Returns insert(table, values) the key (a list for a composite key). values is [[\"Name\",\"Ann\"],[\"Age\",31]] byKey(table, key) the row, or \"\" . The row is tracked : after raw SQL, call discard() to read the new values all(table) all rows where(table, condition, params) rows matching a SQL condition with {0} parameters (SQLite) update(table, key, values) 1 when the row exists, else 0 remove(table, key) 1 when the row existed, else 0 count(table) the number of rows dbValue(row, column) a column of a row ( \"\" for null) Queries # Method Does query(table, spec) rows matchi"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#ringentitydb", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "RingEntityDb", "x": "RingEntityDb # Method Does newEntity(table) a new object of the generated class entityType(table) the .NET Type properties(table) the property names: columns first, then navigations ( Author , Books ) add(entity) adds the entity (and the entities it refers to); returns the key remove(entity) removes it find(table, key) the tracked entity, or \"\" query(table, spec) tracked entities; the specification also accepts [\"include\",[\"Author\",\"Other.Nested\"]] all(table) , count(table, cond) all entities, number of entities getValue(entity, property) , setValue(entity, property, value) read or write a pro"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#query-specification", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Query specification", "x": "Query specification # A query is a list of [item, value] pairs: Item Value where a condition orderby [[column, \"asc\"/\"desc\"], ...] skip , take numbers select [column, ...] \u2014 keeps only those columns (RingDatabase) include [navigation, ...] (RingEntityDb) ignorefilters 1 to ignore the table's global filter A condition is [column, op, value] or a combination: Form Meaning [\"Age\", \">\", 30] comparison: = != < <= > >= [\"Name\", \"contains\", \"an\"] contains , startswith , endswith (text) [\"City\", \"in\", [\"Cairo\",\"Giza\"]] membership [\"Boss\", \"isnull\"] , [\"Boss\", \"notnull\"] null checks [\"and\", c1, c2, ..."}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#providers-and-capabilities", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "Providers and capabilities", "x": "Providers and capabilities # Capability SQLite In-memory SQL Server / PostgreSQL / MySQL tables, rows, relations, queries (expression trees), aggregates \u2714 \u2714 untested where / execute / scalar / sqlRows with SQL \u2714 \u2014 untested migrate / apply \u2714 \u2014 \u2014 transactions \u2714 \u2714 untested typed entities ( RingEntityDb ) \u2714 \u2714 untested"}, {"u": "ef-reference.html#what-is-not-supported", "t": "Entity Framework reference", "h": "What is not supported", "x": "What is not supported # Classes written in C# source \u2014 entity classes are generated from the schema. Lambdas written by hand \u2014 queries are data. Migrations that alter or drop columns (use execute or apply ). Hooks, converters and migrate() in RingEntityDb . The SQL Server, PostgreSQL and MySQL providers have not been tested."}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#two-ways-to-use-an-assembly", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Two ways to use an assembly", "x": "Two ways to use an assembly # Way When Generic calls \u2014 dnLoad , dnType , dnNew , callMethod a quick call, nothing to generate Generated classes \u2014 wrapassembly.ring natural oObj.Method() syntax, properties, events, enums, inheritance"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#generic-calls", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Generic calls", "x": "Generic calls # load \"dotnetlib.ring\" dnLoad(\"path/to/MyLibrary.dll\") # dependencies are found next to the dll oCalc = dnNew(\"MyCompany.Tools.Calculator\",[10]) oCalc.callMethod(\"Add\",[5]) ? oCalc.getProp(\"Total\") # 15 ? dnStatic(\"MyCompany.Tools.Calculator\",\"Square\",[7]) # 49"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#generated-classes", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Generated classes", "x": "Generated classes # Generate Ring classes for every public type of the DLL: cd tools ring wrapassembly.ring path/to/MyLibrary.dll ../myclasses This writes one loader per namespace and one file per class into myclasses/ . Use them like any other wrapped namespace: load \"dotnetlib.ring\" dnLoad(\"path/to/MyLibrary.dll\") dnImportFrom(\"myclasses/\",\"MyCompany.Tools\") oCalc = new Calculator() oCalc.Add(10).Multiply(3) # fluent: methods that return a wrapped class return that class ? oCalc.Total # 30 oCalc.Name = \"mine\" # a property ? new Calculator().Square(7) # a static method ? oCalc.Describe_2(\"Hig"}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#nuget-packages", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "NuGet packages", "x": "NuGet packages # A NuGet package is a set of DLL files. Copy the DLLs of the package (for example from ~/.nuget/packages/<name>/<version>/lib/net8.0/ ) and the DLLs it depends on next to each other, then wrap and load as above: ring wrapassembly.ring Newtonsoft.Json.dll ../json dnLoad(\"Newtonsoft.Json.dll\") dnImportFrom(\"json/\",\"Newtonsoft.Json\") Entity Framework Core is wrapped the same way \u2014 that is how its classes were generated."}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#which-namespaces-to-wrap", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Which namespaces to wrap", "x": "Which namespaces to wrap # wrapassembly.ring <dll> <folder> [namespace ...] wraps only the namespaces you list. Without names, every namespace of the assembly is wrapped."}, {"u": "own-assemblies.html#rules", "t": "Your own assemblies", "h": "Rules", "x": "Rules # Generated loaders use the names of the .NET classes. When a class name is also a name Ring or another namespace already uses, the generator can add a prefix \u2014 see Class generator . Keep the DLL and its dependencies in one folder: assemblies are resolved next to the DLL you load. The runtime mode matters: a DLL that needs Windows Forms requires dnUseDesktop() first."}, {"u": "generator.html", "t": "Class generator", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "generator.html#what-the-generator-does", "t": "Class generator", "h": "What the generator does", "x": "What the generator does # tools/gendotnet.ring reads the manifest ( tools/dotnetmanifest.ring ), loads the assemblies, and uses reflection to write Ring source: libraries/dotnetlib/generated/ System.ring # the namespace loader: loads the class files System/ Math.ring # one file per class Console.ring ... classindex.ring # [type, namespace] of every class: automatic imports Class files are plain Ring: a Class X from Base with a method per .NET method, getX / setX for properties, setXEvent / clearXEvent for events and registration with dnRegisterClass . The files are generated: do not edit them "}, {"u": "generator.html#regenerate", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Regenerate", "x": "Regenerate # cd tools ring gendotnet.ring # every namespace of the manifest ring gendotnet.ring System.Text System.IO # only these"}, {"u": "generator.html#the-manifest", "t": "Class generator", "h": "The manifest", "x": "The manifest # Each entry describes one namespace: Key Meaning :namespace the .NET namespace, also the name used with dnImport(\"...\") :assemblies assemblies that contain the types (default System.Private.CoreLib ) :all true wraps every public class and struct of the namespace :exclude type names that :all must skip :types explicit or extra types: [.NET name, Ring class name, init() parameter count] \u2014 closed generic types and nested types go here :prefer [\"Namespace.Type.Method=arity\"] \u2014 the overload that gets the plain name :uses namespaces imported together with this one (their classes are re"}, {"u": "generator.html#closed-generic-types", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Closed generic types", "x": "Closed generic types # The generic collections have classes through :types . Add your own with a line like: [\"System.Collections.Generic.List`1[[System.Int32]]\",\"IntList\",0] The third item forces the number of parameters of init() (0 here). After regenerating, new IntList() is a List<int> ."}, {"u": "generator.html#name-clashes", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Name clashes", "x": "Name clashes # When two namespaces contain a class with the same short name, the later one gets a prefix: System.Timers.Timer becomes TimersTimer , WPF's Button becomes WpfButton , ASP.NET's MediaTypeHeaderValue becomes AspMediaTypeHeaderValue , EF's clashing names start with Ef ."}, {"u": "generator.html#overload-rules", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Overload rules", "x": "Overload rules # The generator names overloads so that the plain name is the most useful one: the parameterless form wins when the type has one ( Cancel() , Wait() , Reverse() ), except Write* methods; otherwise the shortest overload is the plain name; overloads whose parameters are arrays (except byte[] / char[] ), delegates, comparers or spans never decide the plain name; all other overloads are Name_N where N is the parameter count; ToString() keeps its parameterless form; a method with optional parameters is available with every number of parameters from the required ones up; a static and "}, {"u": "generator.html#how-a-wrapper-class-looks", "t": "Class generator", "h": "How a wrapper class looks", "x": "How a wrapper class looks # Class StringBuilder from Object Func Append P1 return dnRequest(\"call\",[self,\"Append\",P1]) Func getLength return dnRequest(\"get\",[self,\"Length\"]) Func setLength P1 dnRequest(\"set\",[self,\"Length\",P1]) Ring calls getLength() when you read oSB.Length , and setLength(value) when you assign to it."}, {"u": "generator.html#wrapping-your-own-assembly", "t": "Class generator", "h": "Wrapping your own assembly", "x": "Wrapping your own assembly # tools/wrapassembly.ring uses the same generator for a single DLL: Your own assemblies ."}, {"u": "testing.html", "t": "Testing", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "testing.html#the-test-suite", "t": "Testing", "h": "The test suite", "x": "The test suite # sh tests/run.sh B:/ring/bin/ring.exe tests/run.sh runs: every tests/test_*.ring (language bridge, collections, LINQ, system, IO, net, services, web, data, entity framework, keyboard, WPF...), the console samples, every Windows Forms and WPF sample with --selftest , every application with --selftest , and prints 0 failed when everything passes. It needs the package bin folder on the PATH; the script adds it for you. Test file Covers test_foundation.ring , test_runtime.ring the bridge: types, overloads, properties, enums, errors test_collections.ring , test_linq.ring typed colle"}, {"u": "testing.html#dnassert", "t": "Testing", "h": "dnAssert", "x": "dnAssert # load \"dotnetlib.ring\" dnAssert(\"max\",new Math().Max(3,9),9) dnAssert(\"name\",oForm.Text,\"Hello\") dnTestSummary() TESTS: 2 passed, 0 failed A failing assertion prints FAIL: name expected <9> got <3> and counts a failure. dnTestSummary() returns the number of failures."}, {"u": "testing.html#programs-that-test-themselves", "t": "Testing", "h": "Programs that test themselves", "x": "Programs that test themselves # Add three lines to a Windows Forms program and it becomes its own test: with --selftest it shows the form, runs the test code, closes the form and prints the result. oForm = uiForm(\"Click counter\",260,130) ... uiTest(oForm,\"selfTest()\") # runs selfTest() once the form is shown, when --selftest is used uiRun(oForm) # shows the form; the form closes itself after the test uiEnd() # prints SELFTEST OK, or the failures func selfTest oForm.Controls.Item(1).PerformClick() dnAssert(\"one click\",oCount.Text,\"1 clicks\") ring main.ring # the normal program ring main.ring --"}, {"u": "testing.html#typing-keys-in-tests", "t": "Testing", "h": "Typing keys in tests", "x": "Typing keys in tests # uiSendKeys(oForm,\"12{+}30{ENTER}\") types into the focused control of your own form . It posts window messages through the real Windows Forms key processing, so it behaves like a user and can never type into another program (which SendKeys could). Keys: {LEFT} {RIGHT} {UP} {DOWN} {ENTER} {ESC} {BACKSPACE} {TAB} {DELETE} , {+} for a plus sign."}, {"u": "testing.html#testing-the-screenshots", "t": "Testing", "h": "Testing the screenshots", "x": "Testing the screenshots # The screenshots in this documentation are made by docs/tools/shoot_all.sh : it starts each sample, finds its window, captures it with PrintWindow and closes it."}, {"u": "gotchas.html", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": "", "x": ""}, {"u": "gotchas.html#ring-gotchas", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": "Ring gotchas", "x": "Ring gotchas # Use parentheses with new # new StringBuilder(\"x\") \u2014 with parentheses. Without them Ring does not call init() . Strings and numbers # \"text\" + number works. number + \"text\" is an error. Write string(n) + \" items\" . Do not call a method on a new object on the right of an attribute assignment # oForm.Text = new Path().GetFileName(cFile) # Ring loses the assignment! Use a variable first: oPath = new Path() oForm.Text = oPath.GetFileName(cFile) Event code can only see global variables # The code of an event ( setClickEvent(\"...\") ) runs outside the function that set it up. Create the"}, {"u": "gotchas.html#net-gotchas", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": ".NET gotchas", "x": ".NET gotchas # Always call the mode function first # dnUseDesktop() , dnUseWeb() and dnUseAll() must come before any other .NET call : the first call starts the runtime and the mode can not change afterwards. Callbacks run on the Ring thread only # Events from timers on other threads ( System.Threading.Timer , Task.Run(...) ) can not run Ring code; they print a message on stderr and do nothing. Use the Windows Forms Timer ( uiTimer ) or wait for tasks on the Ring thread. The web server queues requests for the same reason. Value types are returned by value # oForm.Location returns a copy of the"}, {"u": "gotchas.html#limits", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": "Limits", "x": "Limits # Not supported Why / what to do Declaring .NET classes or interfaces in Ring Ring is not compiled to .NET. Write them in C#, build a DLL and wrap it . Ring code on .NET threads One Ring thread. Use callbacks only during calls, or queue work as the web server does. ref struct and pointer parameters, Span<T> The bridge converts values, not memory. Use arrays and strings. Lambdas and expression trees written in Ring Callbacks give delegates; EF queries are data ( Entity Framework ). Linux/macOS Windows Forms and WPF Those frameworks are Windows-only. The rest of the bridge is portable but"}, {"u": "gotchas.html#troubleshooting", "t": "Gotchas & limits", "h": "Troubleshooting", "x": "Troubleshooting # Message Cause Runtime Error in loading the dynamic library ring_dotnet.dll is not on the PATH. Add the package bin folder, or install the package into the Ring folder. DotNet Error: No generated classes for the namespace X the name is wrong, or the namespace is not in the manifest \u2014 see Class generator . Error in property name, property not found: x a global variable named like the attribute, or an object that is a plain DotNetObject (use getProp(\"x\") ). Calling function with less number of parameters a constructor that needs arguments: new DateTime(2024,12,25) . Trying to de"}, {"u": "reference.html", "t": "Function index", "h": "", "x": "This page is generated from the comments in libraries/dotnetlib/*.ring . Search it with the box at the top of the page."}, {"u": "reference.html#core-functionsring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Core (functions.ring)", "x": "Core (functions.ring) # Function Description dnRequest(cOperation, aParameters) Send a request to the bridge: dnRequest(\"call\", [oTarget, \"Method\", arg1, ...]), returns one value dnVersion() Version of the .NET runtime that is hosted in this process dnLoad(cPath) Load an assembly (.dll) from a path, returns the assembly name dnLoadName(dn_cName) Load an assembly by name (for example \"System.Xml\") dnType(cTypeName) Get a type (to call its static members), for example dnType(\"System.Math\") dnNew(cTypeName, aParameters) Create a .NET object: dnNew(\"System.Text.StringBuilder\", [\"Hello\"]) dnStatic("}, {"u": "reference.html#dotnetobject-and-friends-classesring", "t": "Function index", "h": "DotNetObject and friends (classes.ring)", "x": "DotNetObject and friends (classes.ring) # Function Description DotNetObject.init(pHandle) (the parameter has another name: \"this.nHandle = ...\" would run Ring's property setter machinery, which breaks an outer assignment like oForm.Text = new Path().GetFileName(...)) DotNetObject.isNull() True for the null reference DotNetObject.callMethod(cMethod, aParameters) Call a method: oObject.callMethod(\"Append\", [\"text\"]) DotNetObject.getProp(cMember) Read a property or a field DotNetObject.setProp(cMember, xValue) Write a property or a field DotNetObject.toString() ToString() of the object DotNetObje"}, {"u": "reference.html#linq-linqring", "t": "Function index", "h": "LINQ (linq.ring)", "x": "LINQ (linq.ring) # Function Description dnQuery(xSource) DotNetQuery.init(xSource) DotNetQuery.Where(cCode) DotNetQuery.Select(cCode) DotNetQuery.SelectMany(cCode) DotNetQuery.OrderBy(cCode) DotNetQuery.OrderByDescending(cCode) DotNetQuery.ThenBy(cCode) DotNetQuery.ThenByDescending(cCode) DotNetQuery.TakeWhile(cCode) DotNetQuery.SkipWhile(cCode) DotNetQuery.Take(nCount) DotNetQuery.Skip(nCount) DotNetQuery.Distinct() DotNetQuery.Reverse() DotNetQuery.Concat(oOther) DotNetQuery.Union(oOther) DotNetQuery.Intersect(oOther) DotNetQuery.Except(oOther) DotNetQuery.ToList() DotNetQuery.Count() DotNet"}, {"u": "reference.html#windows-forms-helpers-guiring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Windows Forms helpers (gui.ring)", "x": "Windows Forms helpers (gui.ring) # Function Description uiForm(cTitle, nWidth, nHeight) A form with a client area of nWidth x nHeight, centered on the screen uiLabel(oParent, cText, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) uiButton(oParent, cText, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight, cClickCode) A button; cClickCode is Ring code run on click (\"\" for none) uiTextBox(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) uiMemo(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) uiCheckBox(oParent, cText, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight, cClickCode) uiListBox(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, nHeight) uiComboBox(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, aItems) uiPanel(oParent, nX, nY, nWidth, "}, {"u": "reference.html#wpf-helpers-wpfring", "t": "Function index", "h": "WPF helpers (wpf.ring)", "x": "WPF helpers (wpf.ring) # Function Description wpfWindow(cTitle, nWidth, nHeight) A window of nWidth x nHeight, centered on the screen wpfXaml(cXaml) Build the objects of a XAML text: wpfXaml('<Button xmlns=\"http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation\" Content=\"Hi\"/>') wpfFind(oRoot, cName) The element called cName (x:Name / Name in XAML) inside oRoot wpfBrush(cColor) A brush from a name or a code: wpfBrush(\"Red\"), wpfBrush(\"#336699\") wpfColor(cColor) A color from a name or a code wpfLabel(cText) wpfText(cText) wpfButton(cText, cClickCode) A button; cClickCode is Ring code run whe"}, {"u": "reference.html#web-server-webring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Web server (web.ring)", "x": "Web server (web.ring) # Function Description webJson(xValue) The response of a handler as JSON: a Ring list, or a list of [name, value] pairs for a JSON object RingRequest.init(pHandle) RingRequest.method() RingRequest.path() RingRequest.queryString() RingRequest.query(cName) a query string value: /search?q=ring --> query(\"q\") RingRequest.body() RingRequest.header(cName) RingRequest.form(cName) RingRequest.setHeader(cName, cValue) RingRequest.context() the ASP.NET HttpContext for everything else RingRequest.respond(nStatus, cType, cBody) WebServer.init(cUrl) WebServer.url() the real address (w"}, {"u": "reference.html#entity-framework-dataring", "t": "Function index", "h": "Entity Framework (data.ring)", "x": "Entity Framework (data.ring) # Function Description dbValue(aRow, cColumn) The value of a column in a row: dbValue(aRow,\"Name\") dbWait(oTask) wait for a Task of the async methods and return its result RingDatabase.init(cConnection, aSchema) cConnection is a SQLite connection string (\"Data Source=people.db\") or starts with the provider: \"inmemory:shop\", \"sqlserver:Server=.;Database=x;...\", \"postgres:Host=...\", \"mysql:Server=...\" (those need the EF Core package of the provider next to RingDotNet.Data.dll) RingDatabase.insert(cTable, aValues) add a row, returns the key RingDatabase.byKey(cTable, "}, {"u": "reference.html#generated-classes", "t": "Function index", "h": "Generated classes", "x": "Generated classes # The wrapper classes are generated; there is one file per class in libraries/dotnetlib/generated/<Namespace>/ . The members of a class are the members of the .NET class (see the .NET documentation ), named by the rules . To see the Ring name of a method, open its class file or call dnDescribe(\"Namespace.Type\") ."}];